# DuDuClaw privileged system helper (duduclaw-sysd) -- Yocto bring-up unit.
#
# Adapted from appliance/mkosi.extra/etc/systemd/system/duduclaw-sysd.service
# (the Debian appliance line's verified unit).
#
# --allowed-uid 0 (Y9-2, 2026-08-27): this line originally shipped with NO
# --allowed-uid flag at all (documented reasoning below, kept verbatim) —
# fail-closed meant duduclaw-sysd bound and listened but denied EVERY
# connection, including the gateway's own. That was confirmed live during
# this ticket's first real `device.update_apply` QEMU run: ESP prepare
# failed with "sysd rejected the call: kind=unauthorized message=caller
# uid is not the configured sysd peer" — not a hang or a crash, exactly
# the documented fail-closed behavior, but it silently blocked the whole
# A/B update chain from ever completing on this line. The Yocto
# duduclaw-gateway.service (recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-cli/files/) also has
# no `User=` yet, so it runs as root (uid 0) same as this unit — `0` is
# the CURRENT real uid the gateway connects as, not a placeholder.
#
# DONE 2026-10-15 (rootless gateway): duduclaw-cli now creates the fixed
# uid 1000 `duduclaw` account and duduclaw-gateway.service runs as it, so
# this unit says `--allowed-uid 1000` (same commit as the user, as planned
# below). sysd chowns the socket to that uid and keeps its own SO_PEERCRED
# check; root (the A/B health check's liveness probe) can still connect
# and gets the structured "unauthorized" reply, which that probe counts as
# alive.
#
# NOT YET: `--require-main-pid-of duduclaw-gateway.service`. Every AI
# employee runs as uid 1000 too, so a uid check alone admits them. The
# platform is adding that flag to duduclaw-sysd (accept a request only from
# the gateway unit's MainPID; it relies on kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=1,
# which duduclaw-cli's 50-duduclaw-ptrace.conf pins). The sysd snapshot
# vendored here (v1.73.1, files/duduclaw-sysd-src) does not know the flag,
# and clap rejects an unknown argument, so adding it now would stop sysd
# from starting at all: no reboot, no update apply, no maintenance mode.
# TODO(platform-sync): when scripts/sync-platform.sh brings in a platform
# snapshot whose `duduclaw-sysd --help` lists --require-main-pid-of, append
# it to ExecStart below in the same commit as that snapshot refresh.
#
# History (Y9-2): the paragraph that follows was written while the gateway
# still ran as root. Running two
# privileged daemons as root and gating them by "root talking to root" is
# not the intended end state (see original reasoning below); `0` is a
# bring-up-stage value that matches today's actual process identities, not
# a security posture to keep past this stage.
#
# Original reasoning (Y2-1) for why omitting the flag was safe to boot with
# at all: duduclaw-sysd's own startup path treats a missing --allowed-uid
# as a legal, fail-closed boot -- it still binds and listens on the socket
# (that ticket's own QEMU acceptance line), it just denies every
# connection until a uid is configured. See crates/duduclaw-sysd/src/
# main.rs's own doc comment: "Omitting BOTH is a legal startup ... denies
# every single connection -- fail-closed, not fail-to-boot."
[Unit]
Description=DuDuClaw privileged system helper (Yocto bring-up)

[Service]
Type=simple
User=root
RuntimeDirectory=duduclaw
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0700
ExecStart=/usr/bin/duduclaw-sysd --allowed-uid 1000
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
StartLimitIntervalSec=300
StartLimitBurst=5

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
